\title{The Rise and Fall of Cross-Factor Predictability: Regime-Dependent HML$\to$SMB Granger Causality}

\author{Chorok Lee}
\authornote{Corresponding author}
\email{chorok.lee@kaist.ac.kr}
\affiliation{%
  \institution{Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology (KAIST)}
  \city{Daejeon}
  \country{South Korea}
}

\begin{abstract}
We document that cross-factor predictive relationships can appear, vanish, and
re-emerge in different market regimes.
Using daily Fama-French data (1990--2024) and a Student-$t$ Hidden Markov Model,
we find that HML Granger-causes SMB in the Normal regime pre-crisis
($p = 8.75 \times 10^{-9}$), but this link undergoes multi-break decay---the
data-driven sup-$F$ maximises at June 1998 ($p = 1.23\times10^{-13}$; LTCM/Russia crisis),
with further deterioration confirmed at January 2008 (Chow $p = 2.29\times10^{-6}$)---and
has been statistically indistinguishable from zero for the subsequent 16 years
(95\% CI $[-0.049, 0.073]$).
A frozen out-of-sample design (HMM trained 1990--2012, applied without refitting
to 2013--2024) reports OOS Elevated re-emergence using two specifications: the raw
Elevated sample yields HAC $p = 0.041$ ($n = 836$), while bootstrap reweighting
to match training-period prevalence yields co-primary HAC $p = 0.153$ (median).
Neither survives the 3-regime Bonferroni correction ($\alpha/3 = 0.0167$).
The OOS effect is small ($\Delta R^2 < 1\%$), does not generate trading profits
(Sharpe $= -0.07$), and is largely attributable to the Elevated regime's post-GFC
expansion from 13.7\% to 30.7\%.
\end{abstract}

