\label{sec:gaussian_comparison}
\textbf{Regime label note.} Under the leakage-safe primary fit, the ``Crisis''
regime captures a high-kurtosis statistical state that does not align with
calendar crises (0\% of 2008, 2011, 2020 days assigned to Crisis vs.\ 83\%
under a Gaussian baseline).\footnote{Table comparing Student-$t$ and Gaussian
crisis detection rates: 2008 Financial (0\% vs.\ 83.3\%), 2011 EU Debt
(0\% vs.\ 25.9\%), 2020 COVID-19 (0\% vs.\ 81.7\%). \label{tab:detection}}
We retain the label for consistency with the HMM
literature~\cite{bulla2011hidden}; the structural break finding is time-indexed
(January 2008), not regime-indexed, and does not depend on this assignment.
The 2008-screened sensitivity fit (seed~42) does align with calendar crises;
we report it separately where noted.

\subsection{The Structural Break in HML$\to$SMB Predictability}
\label{sec:main_result}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Granger Causality Between HML and SMB by Regime. Significance threshold:
$p < 0.00033$ (Bonferroni-corrected for 30 factor pairs).}
\label{tab:main}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
Regime & Direction & $p$-value & Lag & Significant \\
\midrule
Normal & HML $\to$ SMB & $\mathbf{8.75 \times 10^{-9}}$ & 1 & \textbf{Yes} \\
Normal & SMB $\to$ HML & $0.864$ & 1 & No \\
\midrule
Elevated & HML $\to$ SMB & $0.004$ & 1 & Suggestive$^*$ \\
Elevated & SMB $\to$ HML & $0.897$ & 1 & No \\
\midrule
Crisis & HML $\to$ SMB & $0.695$ & 1 & No \\
Crisis & SMB $\to$ HML & $0.294$ & 1 & No \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\footnotesize $^*$Below 0.01 but above Bonferroni threshold $\alpha/30 = 0.00033$.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Key finding under Bonferroni-significant threshold:} only Normal-regime
HML$\to$SMB survives Bonferroni correction.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Normal (Bonferroni-significant):} Strong lag-1 effect
    ($p = 8.75 \times 10^{-9}$), robust to HAC correction. The Granger test conditions
    on lagged SMB, so the HML coefficient reflects \emph{incremental cross-factor}
    predictive content beyond SMB's own history---not a within-SMB autocorrelation artifact.
    \item \textbf{Elevated (suggestive):} HML$\to$SMB is directional at
    conventional levels ($p = 0.004$) but does not pass the paper's
    multiple-testing threshold.
    \item \textbf{Crisis (null):} No detectable lag-1 Granger effect in either
    direction.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Robustness to heteroskedasticity.}
Financial returns exhibit volatility clustering, which may inflate standard Granger
$F$-test significance. We recompute at lag 1 (BIC-optimal) using Newey--West~\cite{newey1987simple}
heteroskedasticity-and-autocorrelation-consistent (HAC) standard errors.
Normal-regime HML$\to$SMB survives HAC correction
($p = 9.45 \times 10^{-8}$ HAC vs.\ $p = 8.75 \times 10^{-9}$ standard).
Elevated HML$\to$SMB weakens under HAC (1990--2024 in-sample: $p = 0.034$), and Crisis remains
non-significant ($p = 0.711$ HAC vs.\ $p = 0.695$ standard).

\textbf{Temporal stability and multi-break decay.}
Pre-2008 Normal ($n = 3{,}140$): $p = 6.66\times10^{-16}$ ($\Delta R^2 = 2.06\%$).
Post-2008 Normal ($n = 1{,}557$): $p = 0.73$ ($\Delta R^2 < 0.01\%$).
(The sum $3{,}140 + 1{,}557 = 4{,}697$ is 26 days fewer than Table~\ref{tab:regimes}'s
Normal total of 4{,}723, due to standard regime-boundary exclusion at lag~1
removing the first observation of each Normal-regime episode.)

\textbf{Primary structural break: June 1998.}
The Quandt-Andrews sup-$F = 21.2$ maximises at \textbf{June 1998} (LTCM/Russia crisis):
$F = 21.20$, $p = 1.23 \times 10^{-13}$.
The top-5 break candidates all cluster in 1998--2003
(June 1998, July 1998, April 1998, August 2003, March 1998),
suggesting the initial weakening of HML$\to$SMB predictability began with
LTCM-driven liquidity stress rather than the GFC.

\textbf{Secondary break: January 2008.}
A theory-motivated Chow test at January 2008 confirms further deterioration:
$F(3,n{-}6) = 9.68$, $p = 2.29\times10^{-6}$; $\hat{\beta}_{\text{HML}}$ shifts
from $-0.189$ (pre-GFC) to $+0.010$ (post-GFC, Wald $z = 5.05$, $p = 9.2\times10^{-7}$).
This is $10^{7}\times$ weaker than the June 1998 break and should be interpreted as
confirming a \emph{later phase} of multi-break structure rather than identifying the
primary breakpoint. Together, the evidence supports gradual erosion of HML$\to$SMB
predictability beginning in the late 1990s, not a single GFC-triggered collapse.
\textbf{Post-break coefficient magnitude.}
The post-2008 coefficient ($\hat{\beta} = 0.012$, SE $= 0.031$, 95\% CI $[-0.049, 0.073]$)
is small relative to the pre-break value ($|\beta_{\text{pre}}| = 0.189$).
A TOST equivalence test~\cite{schuirmann1987comparison} provides
\emph{margin-dependent} evidence: at a margin of $\delta = 0.095$ (50\% of
pre-break effect), $p_{\text{TOST}} = 0.004$; at a stricter margin of
$\delta = 0.05$, $p_{\text{TOST}} = 0.110$ (not significant).
Only margins $\geq 0.10$ (i.e., $\geq 53\%$ of the pre-break effect) achieve $p < 0.05$.
Because the equivalence margin was not pre-specified, we report the 95\% CI
as the primary measure of post-break effect absence and treat the TOST as a
sensitivity analysis rather than a confirmatory test.
Post-GFC attenuation may reflect regulatory constraints
(Volcker Rule reducing proprietary factor trading), factor crowding
(HML--SMB correlation: $\rho_{\text{pre}} = 0.01 \to \rho_{\text{post}} = 0.23$,
absorbing the predictive lead into same-day effects), or multi-break
structure (sup-$F$ at June 1998 hints the single-break Chow test
may not fully resolve the regime transition timing).
These are post-hoc interpretations; the break itself is the finding.

\textbf{Economic magnitude.} \label{tab:r2}
Adding HML lags (1--15) to an SMB autoregression increases $R^2$ by
$\Delta R^2 = 1.04\%$ in Normal ($p = 5.98 \times 10^{-5}$),
1.32\% in Elevated ($p = 0.002$), and 0.56\% in Crisis ($p = 0.988$).
Pre-GFC Normal $\Delta R^2 = 2.06\%$---modest but typical for daily
factor predictability~\cite{ehsani2022factor}. These magnitudes confirm
statistically significant predictive content without implying
trading profitability (see Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/granger_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{All-pairs Granger causality by regime ($-\log_{10}(p)$, lag=5).
Bordered cells with asterisks survive Bonferroni correction.
The strongest regime-differential pair is MKT-RF$\to$SMB; HML$\to$SMB appears as
a secondary differential pattern.}
\Description{Three heatmaps of pairwise Granger significance by regime; darker cells indicate lower p-values, with Bonferroni-surviving cells marked.}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} shows Granger causality $p$-values for all 30
directed factor pairs across three regimes (lag~5). HML$\to$SMB is one of
several regime-sensitive pairs; its distinctive feature is the structural
break documented above.

\subsection{Economic Interpretation and Supporting Evidence}
\label{sec:interpretation}
\label{sec:overlap_evidence}
\label{sec:oos}
We hypothesize a deleveraging cascade channel: forced unwinding of value positions
simultaneously deleverages associated size exposures, creating a predictive lag from
HML to SMB (Appendix~\ref{app:mechanism}). FF25 portfolio overlap analysis (sensitivity
fit, seed~42) finds Granger significance concentrating in small-cap portfolios
($\rho_s = 0.35$, permutation $p = 0.046$; Appendix~\ref{app:overlap}), consistent
with this channel. Event-level validation across six stress episodes shows the expected
pattern in 2 of 6 events, with 4 of 6 directionally correct---heterogeneous and low-power
(Appendix~\ref{app:events}).
Alternative explanations (common drivers, information propagation) cannot be ruled out.

\subsection{Machine Learning Validation and Nonlinear Asymmetry}
\label{sec:ml_validation}

To validate the linearity of the forward HML$\to$SMB channel and probe for
residual complexity, we apply multiple model classes following the
Tank et al.~\cite{tank2022neural} neural Granger protocol.
Rather than seeking improved prediction, we use ML as a \emph{diagnostic
lens}: if nonlinear methods detect the same causal direction as the
linear Granger test, the finding is robust to functional form;
if they fail where linear methods succeed, the signal is genuinely linear.

\textbf{LSTM attention reveals regime-dependent lag structure.}
An attention-mechanism LSTM~\cite{tank2022neural} (32 hidden units,
temporal attention over 9 lags) trained per regime exposes which lag
distances carry predictive content (Figure~\ref{fig:ml_diagnostic},
top row). In the Normal regime, 68.2\% of attention concentrates on
lag~1---a sharp peak consistent with the lag-1 BIC selection
(Table~\ref{tab:main}). This concentration decays monotonically across
regimes: 52.9\% in Elevated, 44.2\% in Crisis (approaching the uniform
baseline of 11.1\%), mirroring the multi-break decay of the Granger
signal. The attention gradient provides a model-free confirmation that
the structural break is mechanism-level, not an artifact of test specification.

\textbf{Random Forest importance confirms linear lag dominance.}
A four-model diagnostic (OLS, RF with 100 trees, MLP with 64-32 architecture,
LSTM; full details in Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}) finds that
\emph{no nonlinear method improves prediction in any regime}
(Table~\ref{tab:neural}; RF $p > 0.13$, MLP $p > 0.20$, LSTM $p > 0.63$
under the primary fit). RF permutation importance
(Figure~\ref{fig:ml_diagnostic}, bottom row) shows HML lag-1 as the
dominant feature in the Crisis regime (importance $= 0.043$,
$4\times$ the mean), while Normal-regime HML lags are diffuse---consistent
with the linear Granger test capturing all available predictive content.

\textbf{Transfer entropy reveals nonlinear reverse channel.}
While linear methods detect only forward HML$\to$SMB, transfer
entropy~\cite{schreiber2000measuring,barnett2009granger} (Frenzel--Pompe
kNN, $k = 5$, 200 permutations; Figure~\ref{fig:te_asymmetry})
reveals the \emph{reverse} channel SMB$\to$HML is nonlinear and
substantially stronger in the Normal regime ($z = 5.37$, $p < 10^{-6}$
vs.\ forward $z = 2.45$, $p = 0.007$; Table~\ref{tab:te}).
This asymmetry---linear forward, nonlinear reverse---reconciles the null
reverse Granger ($p = 0.864$) with highly significant reverse TE:
the reverse channel is real but invisible to linear tests.
Both directions collapse in the Crisis regime ($z < 1.3$, $p > 0.11$),
confirming the structural break destroys all predictive structure.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/ml_attention_importance.pdf}
\caption{ML diagnostics by regime. \emph{Top:} LSTM attention weights
over 9 lags (dashed line $= 1/9$ uniform baseline). Lag-1 concentration
decays from 68.2\% (Normal) to 44.2\% (Crisis), mirroring the Granger
structural break. \emph{Bottom:} Random Forest permutation importance
for SMB lags (blue) vs.\ HML lags (red). HML lag-1 dominates in Crisis;
Normal-regime HML importance is diffuse.}
\Description{Six-panel bar chart: top row shows LSTM attention weights by lag for Normal, Elevated, and Crisis regimes; bottom row shows RF feature importance for SMB and HML lags by regime.}
\label{fig:ml_diagnostic}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/te_asymmetry_by_regime.pdf}
\caption{Transfer entropy directional asymmetry. Forward HML$\to$SMB
(blue) is modest; reverse SMB$\to$HML (red) dominates in Normal
($z = 5.37^{***}$). Both collapse in Crisis. Stars denote
permutation significance ($^{*}p < 0.05$, $^{**}p < 0.01$,
$^{***}p < 0.001$).}
\Description{Grouped bar chart of transfer entropy z-scores by regime and direction, showing reverse SMB-to-HML dominance in Normal regime.}
\label{fig:te_asymmetry}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Frozen-Parameter Out-of-Sample Test (Primary Validation)}
\label{sec:frozen_oos}

Two-stage approaches risk circularity: the HMM uses the same returns for
regime assignment that Granger tests subsequently analyze.
We resolve this by fitting the Student-$t$ HMM exclusively on 1990--2012
($n = 5{,}797$ days), freezing all parameters
($\boldsymbol{\mu}_k$, $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k$, $\nu_k$, $\mathbf{A}$),
and classifying 2013--2024 ($n = 3{,}020$ days) without refitting.
Granger tests are then run on the held-out labels using the same
\texttt{select\_lag\_bic} + \texttt{run\_granger\_at\_lag} pipeline as Table~\ref{tab:main},
ensuring full methodological consistency.

The frozen HMM assigns 24.0\% to Normal, 30.7\% to Elevated, and 45.3\%
to Crisis in the held-out period (vs.\ 21.5\%, 13.7\%, 64.8\% in training).
Notably, the Elevated-regime share more than doubles from 13.7\% (training)
to 30.7\% (test), consistent with the OOS Elevated significance.
After lag-1 boundary exclusion (same rule as Section~\ref{sec:granger_method}),
the OOS retains 661$+$836$+$1{,}299$=2{,}796$ of 3,020 days (7.4\% excluded).
The OOS exclusion rate (7.4\%) is higher than the full-sample lag-1 Granger exclusion
(0.67\%; 59/8,817), reflecting more frequent regime transitions when the
frozen 1990--2012 classifier is applied to the unseen 2013--2024 period.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\caption{Frozen OOS Granger Causality (HML$\to$SMB), 2013--2024.
HMM trained on 1990--2012 only; relabeling uses train-period ordering;
lag $= 1$ pre-fixed from in-sample BIC; filtered probabilities (no future info).
HAC-$p$ uses Andrews (1991) automatic bandwidth selection (AR(1) plug-in, bandwidth=2); Elevated HAC $p = 0.043$.
\textbf{Caveat:} Bootstrap prevalence reweighting (matching training-period Elevated prevalence
of 13.7\%) yields median $p = 0.153$; only 9.9\% of subsamples achieve $p < 0.05$.}
\label{tab:frozen_granger}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Regime & $n$ & $F$-$p$ & HAC-$p$ & $\Delta R^2$ \\
\midrule
Normal    & 661 & 0.149 & 0.153 & 0.32\% \\
Elevated  & 836 & \textbf{0.014} & \textbf{0.043} & \textbf{0.73\%} \\
Crisis    & 1,299 & 0.281 & 0.290 & 0.09\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Result} (Table~\ref{tab:frozen_granger}). HML$\to$SMB shows suggestive evidence in the Elevated regime
($p = 0.014$; HAC $p = 0.043$ via Andrews automatic bandwidth; $\Delta R^2 = 0.73\%$ at lag~1),
while Normal ($p = 0.149$) and Crisis ($p = 0.281$) are null.
\textbf{However, this result is prevalence-dependent:} bootstrap reweighting to match
training-period Elevated prevalence (13.7\% vs.\ 30.7\% in the test period)
yields median $p = 0.153$, with only 9.9\% of subsamples significant at $p < 0.05$.
The Elevated regime's post-GFC expansion is the primary driver of significance.
\emph{Convention note:} The $n = 836$ in Table~\ref{tab:frozen_granger} uses the
decimal-unit pipeline; the permutation test uses percentage-unit ($n = 953$), yielding
slightly different regime assignments. Both conventions yield $F$-$p < 0.05$.
This result does not survive Bonferroni correction across 30 pairs
(threshold $\alpha/30 = 0.00033$, family-wise $\alpha = 0.01$); its evidential weight
rests on the economic motivation for the HML--SMB pair
(Section~\ref{sec:pairselection}).
Notably, in-sample significance is expected given the degrees of freedom consumed
by regime identification, lag selection, and pair screening on the same data;
the OOS result is therefore the more informative---if more modest---test of
generalization.
A full 50-seed sensitivity analysis across all 7 local optima clusters finds that
\textbf{all 7 clusters} show Elevated significance ($F$-$p < 0.05$ and HAC-$p < 0.05$
in each), including the lowest-LL cluster (seed~35, 15 seeds; Elevated HAC-$p = 0.026$).
This represents robustness across 7 distinct HMM solutions spanning the full
range of local optima (BIC 75{,}587--76{,}137).
All results use forward-only (filtered) probabilities and train-period relabeling,
eliminating look-ahead.
\textbf{Power analysis.} At $n = 836$, the OOS test has 98.7\% power to detect
the in-sample effect size ($\Delta R^2 = 2.06\%$) and 69.7\% at the observed
OOS magnitude ($\Delta R^2 = 0.73\%$). The smaller OOS effect reflects genuine
attenuation, not low power.

\textbf{Regime redistribution.} The in-sample Normal dominance vs.\ OOS Elevated
significance reflects the frozen classifier redistributing regime mass:
Elevated grows from 13.7\% (training) to 30.7\% (test), concentrating the
HML$\to$SMB signal in this regime.

\textbf{Prevalence reweighting test.}
The Elevated regime expands from 13.7\% (training) to 30.7\% (test),
raising the concern that significance is mechanically driven by the larger sample.
We conduct a bootstrap subsampling test: for each of 1{,}000 iterations, we randomly
subsample the OOS Elevated observations to match the training-period prevalence
(target $n = 697$) and re-estimate the Granger $F$-statistic.
Result: median bootstrap $p = 0.153$; only 9.9\% of subsamples achieve $p < 0.05$.
This suggests the OOS Elevated significance is partially driven by the regime expansion
and may not generalise at the training-period prevalence level.
We retain the full-sample OOS result as our primary specification but acknowledge
this prevalence dependence as a limitation.

Event-level OOS behavior is heterogeneous across held-out stress episodes
(Appendix~\ref{app:events}), highlighting that the per-regime pooled result
captures the aggregate signal more reliably than individual events.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{The Structural Break: Robustness of the Main Finding}
\label{sec:robustness}
\label{sec:msvar}
\label{sec:trading}
\label{sec:risk_monitoring}

The pre-crisis Normal-regime finding ($p = 8.75 \times 10^{-9}$, multi-break
confirmed at June 1998 and January 2008) is robust across multiple dimensions.
HAC bandwidth sensitivity: the Andrews~\cite{andrews1991heteroskedasticity} automatic bandwidth
(AR(1) plug-in for Bartlett kernel) yields bandwidth $= 2$ for the OOS Elevated sample
(residual $\hat{\rho} = -0.022$), which is our primary specification; the Newey--West default is bandwidth $= 6$;
a rule-of-thumb gives bandwidth $= 7$ (Table~\ref{tab:bandwidth_sensitivity}).
Results are robust across all bandwidths tested (1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 10, 15, 20, 30):
HAC $p$-values range from 0.041 (bandwidth~1) to 0.173 (bandwidth~30),
with all bandwidths $\leq 15$ yielding $p < 0.05$.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{HAC Bandwidth Sensitivity: OOS Elevated HML$\to$SMB $p$-values.
Primary specification: Andrews (1991) automatic AR(1) plug-in (bandwidth~$B = 2$).}
\label{tab:bandwidth_sensitivity}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lc@{}}
\toprule
Bandwidth & HAC $p$-value \\
\midrule
$B = 1$ (Granger lag) & 0.041 \\
$B = 2$ (Andrews automatic) \textbf{[PRIMARY]} & \textbf{0.043} \\
$B = 4$ & 0.048 \\
$B = 6$ (NW default) & 0.056 \\
$B = 10$ & 0.078 \\
$B = 15$ & 0.120 \\
$B = 20$ & 0.148 \\
$B = 30$ & 0.173 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Conditioning on one lag of MKT-RF, the in-sample Normal signal remains
Bonferroni-significant, and MKT-RF itself is non-significant (F-$p > 0.43$).
BIC selects lag~1 for all regime$\times$direction combinations; significance
is robust across lags 1--15 in Normal (Figure~\ref{fig:lag_sensitivity}).
Rolling 3-year unconditional Granger tests (Figure~\ref{fig:rolling}) show
episodic peaks during stress periods (2008--2009, 2020), consistent with
the regime-conditional finding. A four-model diagnostic (OLS, RF, MLP, LSTM;
Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}) finds no nonlinear improvement for forward
HML$\to$SMB, confirming the linear Granger test captures the full predictive
content. The post-2008 absence ($p = 0.73$) is scope definition, not a
limitation: it demonstrates a clean structural break.

