\Description{Line chart of HML-to-SMB p-values by lag for each regime, highlighting strong Normal-regime significance and weak Crisis-regime evidence.}
\label{fig:lag_sensitivity}
\end{figure}

\subsection{OOS Re-Emergence: Secondary Evidence with Disclosed Fragility}

The frozen OOS Elevated result is exploratory supporting evidence, not a
co-equal finding. It is simultaneously sensitive to four dimensions:
(i)~\emph{prevalence:} bootstrap reweighting to training-period Elevated prevalence
yields median $p = 0.153$ (only 9.9\% of subsamples significant),
indicating that the regime expansion from 13.7\% to 30.7\% drives most of the
significance;
(ii)~\emph{scale convention:} permutation $p \in [0.063, 0.022]$;
(iii)~\emph{local optima:} 7 of 7 significant (Appendix~\ref{app:optima});
(iv)~\emph{regime count:} null at $K = 2$, marginal at $K = 4$
(Table~\ref{tab:k_sensitivity}).
We present it as fragile, prevalence-dependent evidence of regime-conditional
re-emergence, informative because it uses frozen parameters and held-out data,
but not confirmatory.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{$K$-Sensitivity: Frozen OOS HML$\to$SMB by Number of Regimes.
Permutation: 5{,}000 label shuffles (percentage-unit convention).}
\label{tab:k_sensitivity}
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{@{}lclcccc@{}}
\toprule
$K$ & $\Delta$BIC$_{K\text{vs.}3}$ & Best Regime & $n$ & F-$p$ & Perm.\ $p$ & Sig.? \\
\midrule
2 & $+1{,}680$ & High-Vol & 1{,}917 & 0.514 & 0.572 & No \\
\textbf{3} & \textbf{---} & \textbf{Elevated} & \textbf{953} & \textbf{0.003} & \textbf{0.025} & \textbf{Yes} \\
4 & $+312$ & Low-Elev. & 855 & 0.056 & --- & No \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Pair selection transparency.}
MOM$\to$SMB---the top-ranked pair by OOS $F$-statistic (rank 1/30,
$F = 20.3$)---shows the same regime-dependent pattern (permutation
$p = 0.010$, $\Delta R^2 = 1.94\%$). Our primary pair selection is guided
by an economic prior (HML--SMB institutional crowding) rather than pure
empirical strength. This reduces degrees of freedom but introduces potential
prior-driven selection; pre-registered replication on international data would
provide definitive confirmation.
Additional robustness (subsample stability, MS-VAR, weekly aggregation,
transfer entropy, filtered vs.\ smoothed) is in Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}.

\subsection{Scope, Interpretation, and Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\textbf{Causal interpretation.}
All findings document \emph{predictive precedence} (see Section~\ref{sec:granger_method} for causal scope).
A common driver affecting both factors with different lags cannot be ruled out.
The deleveraging hypothesis (Appendix~\ref{app:mechanism}) is plausible but
unverified; holdings-level 13F verification remains for future work.

\textbf{Economic magnitude.}
Effect sizes are small ($\Delta R^2 \approx 2\%$ pre-GFC) and do not generate
trading profits (Sharpe $= -0.07$; Appendix~\ref{app:trading}). The value is
academic: factor-timing models that assume regime-invariant cross-factor
relationships may misspecify dynamics during structural transitions.

\section{Conclusion}

Cross-factor predictive structure is regime-dependent and can undergo
multi-break decay. HML$\to$SMB Granger causality was Bonferroni-significant
in the pre-crisis Normal regime ($p = 8.75 \times 10^{-9}$, $\Delta R^2 = 2.06\%$),
with the data-driven sup-$F$ identifying June 1998 as the primary structural break
($p = 1.23\times10^{-13}$) and January 2008 confirming further deterioration
(Chow $p = 2.29\times10^{-6}$). The post-2008 coefficient is statistically
indistinguishable from zero for 16 years (95\% CI $[-0.049, 0.073]$).
Frozen OOS testing finds prevalence-dependent evidence
of re-emergence in the Elevated regime (HAC $p = 0.041$; bootstrap prevalence-matched
$p = 0.153$), though this does not survive Bonferroni correction
and is largely attributable to regime expansion (13.7\%$\to$30.7\%). A secondary pair
(MOM$\to$SMB, $p = 0.010$) extends the pattern beyond a single factor pair.

These findings imply that factor-timing models assuming regime-invariant
cross-factor relationships may misspecify dynamics during structural
transitions. Future work should pursue pre-registered replication on
international factor data, Bayesian model averaging over $K$, and
holdings-level verification of the deleveraging channel via 13F filings.

